  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/....\nThe weather pattern for next week will see the subtropical high\nbecoming the dominate feature across the Southwestern U.S. while\nits strength will barely change. Guidance agrees the ridge center\nwill shift from Texas on Monday to somewhere over Arizona on\nTuesday with H5 heights rising to 595-597dm. H5 heights of this\nmagnitude will represent the 97th percentile of climatology for \nthe period and temperatures will follow with forecast highs only \nfalling short of daily records by a few degrees starting Monday. \nSince moisture levels are now expected to remain fairly limited \nfor much if not all of next week, the day-to-day convective \npotential should remain quite low and likely confined to far \neastern Arizona. The most likely scenario keeps the ridge in full \ncontrol over the Desert Southwest with the center hovering \nsomewhere over Arizona and/or into western New Mexico.\n\nForecast confidence for a more prolonged heat event has increased\nleading us to expand the Extreme Heat Watch through southwest \nArizona and much of southeast California, while extending the \ntimeframe through next Friday. NBM forecast highs show readings \nanywhere from 108-113 degrees across the lower deserts each day \nnext week with overnight lows inching toward the 90 degree mark as\nearly as Tuesday as surface dew points shift a bit higher.\nForecast Major HeatRisk has also expanded more across the western\nlower deserts going into the middle part of next week with the\nbulk of the area keeping Major HeatRisk levels through at least \nnext Friday. There is uncertainty in the length of time this heat \nevent will last as some of the guidance shows the ridge center \nmay eventually shift more to the north. If this occurs, it should\nhelp to increase the amount of monsoon activity over the region, \neventually leading to some degree of cooling.",
